For a Coxeter system $(W,S)$, we define the boolean complex of involutions as a counterpart to the classical boolean complex of $W$, which was introduced by Adiprasito, Nevo, and Valdes-Yanez. When the associated rank $n$ is odd, we demonstrate that this new complex shares the same homotopy type with the boolean sphere $B_{n-1}$. Conversely, if $n$ is even, the homotopy type corresponds to that of the suspension of $B_{n-1}$. Furthermore, under the assumption that $W$ possesses a specific geometric representation, we show that the boolean complex of all involutions aligns with a section of the corresponding complex of subrepresentations. Finally, for the symmetric group $S_d$, we establish that the $i$-th skeleton of this complex has the homotopy type of a sphere $S_{d-i-1}$. These findings extend earlier work by Bjorner and Wachs, as well as Valdes-Yanez. Additionally, we provide a geometric description of the boundary of this complex and analyze its connection to the classical Deligne complex. All relevant proofs and theoretical discussions are available at the following address: http://arxiv.org/abs/1509.00701.

